<header><p class="st-kicker">county · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Gower</h1><p class="st-meta">1106 CE – 1536 CE (approx.) · capital Swansea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A marcher lordship on the Gower peninsula and around Swansea, created by Henry de Beaumont, Earl of Warwick, in the early 12th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-de-beaumont-1st-earl-of-warwick">Henry de Beaumont</a>, Earl of Warwick, took Gower from its Welsh rulers around 1106 and built a castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swansea">Swansea</a>. English and Flemish settlers occupied the southern part of the peninsula, which developed a lasting English-speaking character.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Gower was divided between an Anglicised lowland '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/englishry">Englishry</a>' and a Welsh upland '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welshry">Welshry</a>'. The lords held their own courts and granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swansea">Swansea</a> its first charter. The lordship passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braose-family">de Braose family</a> and later to the Mowbrays and the Herberts, and was repeatedly fought over by the lords of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-glamorgan">Glamorgan</a> and Welsh princes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laws-in-wales-acts-1535-and-1542">Laws in Wales Act</a> of 1536 attached Gower to the new county of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-glamorgan">Glamorgan</a>. Its distinct dialect and Anglo-Norman churches remain, and in 1956 it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain's</a> first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/area-of-outstanding-natural-beauty">Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-gower.jpg" alt="Lordship of Gower" width="320" height="250"><figcaption>Image: LinguisticDemographer at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LDGowerLordship.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1106 CE – 1536 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Swansea</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry de Beaumont, William de Braose</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Welsh, Middle English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gower</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1321&amp;focus=lordship-of-gower" aria-label="Lordship of Gower on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4591 390 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Gower lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4911 646L4970 663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4911 646h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4911 646h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1321&amp;focus=lordship-of-gower">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
